    ## 1. Quickstart: Central Server (Ubuntu)
    
    Run the central PALANTIR platform on your primary server:
    
    ```bash
    # 1. Clone repository and set up environment
    git clone https://github.com/abdullah-farooqi/PALANTIR.git
    cd PALANTIR
    cp .env.example .env
    
    # Generate separate values for each configured token, then set them in .env.
    # Configure CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS if the UI uses a different origin.
    # Repeat the command and use distinct values for read, admin, and enrollment tokens.
    python3 -c 'import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(48))'
    
    # 2. Launch the full stack
    docker compose up -d
    
    # 3. Verify health
    curl -s http://localhost:8000/healthz | python3 -m json.tool
    ```
    
    On boot, the backend probes the configured Netdata endpoint, discovers the contexts available on that host, and registers `local-node` into PostgreSQL. Context counts are host-specific and must not be hard-coded.
    
    ### API access
    
    All `/api/v1` routes require a Bearer token. Read tokens can only call read
    routes; admin tokens can also deactivate nodes and request investigations.
    The optional enrollment token can create a node or repeat an identical
    enrollment, but cannot modify or reactivate an existing node. Configure distinct
    random tokens of at least 32 characters in the central `.env`; API access fails
    closed if none are configured. Keep the shared tokens on the server. A trusted
    reverse proxy should authenticate users, discard incoming `Authorization`
    headers, and inject the appropriate token. Do not place these tokens in the
    React bundle. Set `CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS` to comma-separated explicit origins
    when the UI is hosted on a different origin; same-origin deployments can leave
    it empty.
    
