        69    On boot, the backend probes the configured Netdata endpoint, discovers the contexts available on that host, and registers `local-node` into PostgreSQL. Context counts are host-specific and must not be hard-coded.
        70
        71    ### API access
        72
        73    All `/api/v1` routes require a Bearer token. Read tokens can call read routes;
        74    admin tokens can also deactivate nodes and request investigations. The optional
        75    enrollment token can create a node or repeat an identical enrollment, but cannot
        76    modify or reactivate one. Tokens are distinct, random values of at least 32
        77    characters and stay in the server `.env`. Caddy removes browser-supplied
        78    authorization and identity headers, then injects a role-scoped token after
        79    Authentik authenticates the session. The only caller-supplied token accepted at
        80    the proxy is the restricted enrollment token, and only for `POST /api/v1/nodes`;
        81    the backend further limits it to create-only registration. The backend listens
        82    on host loopback, so LAN clients use the proxy. Never put shared tokens in
        83    browser JavaScript.
        84
        85    ### LAN proxy and Authentik setup
        86
        87    The included Caddy and Authentik services use `palantir.home.arpa` and
        88    `auth.palantir.home.arpa`. Add both names to the LAN DNS server (or each client’s
        89    hosts file), pointing them to the central server’s static LAN IP. Keep Caddy
        90    bound to that LAN interface and allow ports 80/443 only from the LAN. The
        91    `home.arpa` suffix is reserved for residential home networks; it is not a public
        92    DNS name.
