The API output is large because it includes multiple recent snapshots. For a table with one latest snapshot per category and source, run this on the central laptop. It selects the latest snapshot at or before the example time; replace that timestamp with another UTC time, or use `now()` for the latest available data.

```bash
docker exec -i palantir-postgres psql -U palantir -d palantir -P pager=off <<'SQL'
WITH params AS (
    SELECT TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-10-07 09:12:41+00' AS as_of
),
latest AS (
    SELECT DISTINCT ON (
        m.category, COALESCE(m.data->>'source', 'netdata')
    )
        m.collected_at,
        m.category,
        COALESCE(m.data->>'source', 'netdata') AS source,
        m.data
    FROM monitored_nodes n
    JOIN metric_snapshots m ON m.node_id = n.id
    WHERE n.hostname = 'rawal-lab1-pc-01'
      AND m.collected_at <= (SELECT as_of FROM params)
    ORDER BY
        m.category,
        COALESCE(m.data->>'source', 'netdata'),
        m.collected_at DESC,
        m.id DESC
),
payloads AS (
    SELECT
        collected_at,
        category,
        source,
        CASE
            WHEN source = 'palantir-agent'
                THEN COALESCE(data->'data', '{}'::jsonb)
            ELSE data - 'source'
        END AS payload
    FROM latest
)
SELECT
    collected_at AT TIME ZONE 'UTC' AS collected_at_utc,
    category,
    source,
    item.metric,
    item.value
FROM payloads
CROSS JOIN LATERAL jsonb_each(
    CASE
        WHEN jsonb_typeof(payload) = 'object' THEN payload
        ELSE jsonb_build_object('value', payload)
    END
) AS item(metric, value)
ORDER BY collected_at_utc DESC, category, source, metric;
SQL
```

This produces rows in the form `timestamp | category | source | metric | value`. Nested fields, such as the container list, stay as JSON in the value column. For just one category through the API, use `/api/v1/nodes/<node-id>/metrics/containers?limit=1`; it returns the latest full snapshot and its collection time.

